var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"02-a1b2c3d4","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM policies: identity vs resource-based","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Lambda function in Account A needs to read objects from an S3 bucket in Account B. Which combination of policies is required?","options":{"A":"Identity-based policy on the Lambda execution role only","B":"Resource-based policy (S3 bucket policy) on the bucket in Account B granting access to the Lambda execution role in Account A, AND an identity-based policy on the Lambda execution role allowing s3:GetObject","C":"Resource-based policy on the S3 bucket only","D":"SCP in Account B's AWS Organization allowing cross-account S3 access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For cross-account access to S3, both sides must grant permission. The S3 bucket policy (resource-based) must explicitly allow the Lambda execution role from Account A. The Lambda execution role (identity-based) must also allow s3:GetObject. Either policy alone is insufficient for cross-account access — both must agree.","distractors":{"A":"An identity-based policy on the role in Account A alone is insufficient; the resource in Account B must also explicitly grant access.","C":"A bucket policy alone authorizing Account A works only if the role in Account A also has the permission in its identity-based policy; AWS requires both for cross-account.","D":"SCPs restrict what accounts can do but cannot grant cross-account resource access; they are guardrails, not grants."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_identity-vs-resource.html","tags":["iam","cross-account","s3","resource-policy","identity-policy"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b2c3d4e5","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM policies: identity vs resource-based","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which of the following is an example of a resource-based policy in AWS?","options":{"A":"An IAM policy attached to an IAM user allowing s3:PutObject","B":"An S3 bucket policy granting public read access to all objects","C":"An IAM permission boundary attached to an IAM role","D":"An AWS Organizations SCP restricting EC2 instance types"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A resource-based policy is attached directly to the resource (the S3 bucket in this case) rather than to an IAM identity. S3 bucket policies, SQS queue policies, KMS key policies, and Lambda resource-based policies are all examples. They specify who (principal) can do what to that specific resource.","distractors":{"A":"A policy attached to an IAM user is an identity-based policy, not a resource-based policy.","C":"A permission boundary is an IAM feature that limits the maximum permissions of an identity; it is a special type of identity-based policy management tool.","D":"SCPs are AWS Organizations policies that restrict what accounts can do; they are not resource-based policies on individual AWS resources."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies_identity-vs-resource.html","tags":["iam","resource-based-policy","identity-based-policy"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c3d4e5f6","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM policies: identity vs resource-based","difficulty":"hard","question":"An IAM role has an identity-based policy denying s3:DeleteObject. A bucket policy on the same account's S3 bucket has an Allow for s3:DeleteObject for that role. Can the role delete objects?","options":{"A":"Yes — the bucket policy Allow overrides the identity-based Deny","B":"No — an explicit Deny in any applicable policy overrides all Allows","C":"Yes — resource-based policies take precedence over identity-based policies in the same account","D":"No — the two policies cancel each other out, resulting in an implicit deny"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS IAM evaluation logic: an explicit Deny always overrides any Allow, regardless of which policy type contains it. If the identity-based policy has an explicit Deny for s3:DeleteObject, the role cannot delete objects even if the S3 bucket policy grants Allow. The only exception is the root account, which cannot be denied by IAM policies (but can be by SCPs).","distractors":{"A":"Resource-based policies do not override explicit Denies in identity-based policies; the Deny evaluation happens before Allow in the policy evaluation order.","C":"There is no precedence rule where resource-based policies override identity-based policies; all applicable policies are evaluated together and an explicit Deny wins.","D":"The policies don't 'cancel out'; the explicit Deny wins definitively — the result is a Deny, not an implicit deny."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html","tags":["iam","policy-evaluation","explicit-deny"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d4e5f6a7","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM policies: identity vs resource-based","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company wants to ensure that only requests made through a specific VPC endpoint can access their S3 bucket. Which policy type enforces this?","options":{"A":"IAM identity-based policy with a condition on the source VPC","B":"S3 bucket policy (resource-based) with an aws:sourceVpce condition denying all other access","C":"SCP restricting S3 access to the VPC CIDR range","D":"VPC endpoint policy restricting outbound traffic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An S3 bucket policy can use the aws:sourceVpce condition key to restrict access to requests originating from a specific VPC endpoint. By adding a Deny for all principals when aws:sourceVpce does not match the target endpoint, all requests not coming through that VPC endpoint are denied, regardless of IAM permissions.","distractors":{"A":"Identity-based policies on individual users or roles are difficult to manage at scale and don't cover all possible identities; the bucket policy approach centrally enforces the constraint at the resource.","C":"SCPs restrict what actions member accounts can take but cannot enforce network-level conditions like VPC endpoint requirements.","D":"VPC endpoint policies control what the endpoint allows outbound, but the enforcement on the S3 side requires the bucket policy condition."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-bucket-policies-vpc-endpoint.html","tags":["iam","s3","vpc-endpoint","bucket-policy","condition-key"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e5f6a7b8","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM roles vs users: when to use each","difficulty":"easy","question":"An EC2 instance needs to call DynamoDB APIs. What is the AWS-recommended way to grant it access?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM user, generate access keys, and store them in /etc/environment on the instance","B":"Attach an IAM instance profile (role) to the EC2 instance with the required DynamoDB permissions","C":"Hard-code AWS access keys in the application configuration file","D":"Use the root account credentials stored in AWS Secrets Manager and fetched at startup"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM instance profiles attach an IAM role to an EC2 instance. The AWS SDK automatically retrieves temporary credentials from the instance metadata service (IMDS) — no key management required. Credentials rotate automatically. This is the AWS best practice: never store long-term credentials on EC2 instances.","distractors":{"A":"Storing IAM user access keys on EC2 instances creates long-term credentials that must be manually rotated; if the instance is compromised, the keys are exposed indefinitely.","C":"Hard-coded credentials are the worst practice — they end up in version control, logs, and AMI snapshots, creating persistent security exposure.","D":"Using root credentials is a critical security violation; root should have MFA enabled and never be used programmatically."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_use_switch-role-ec2.html","tags":["iam","instance-profile","roles","ec2","credentials"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f6a7b8c9","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM roles vs users: when to use each","difficulty":"medium","question":"A third-party auditing company needs read-only access to your AWS account for a one-time audit. They have their own AWS account. Which approach follows AWS best practices?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM user in your account with read-only permissions and share the credentials with the auditors","B":"Create an IAM role in your account with read-only permissions and a trust policy allowing the auditor's AWS account to assume it","C":"Give the auditors your root account credentials with MFA enabled","D":"Create an IAM user with a time-limited password that expires after the audit"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Creating an IAM role with a trust policy that allows the auditor's AWS account to assume it follows the principle of least privilege and does not require sharing long-term credentials. The auditors use their own IAM identities to assume the role and receive temporary credentials. Access is revoked by deleting the role or updating the trust policy.","distractors":{"A":"Creating an IAM user with shared credentials creates long-term credentials that must be manually revoked; if forgotten, they remain active indefinitely.","C":"Never share root credentials with external parties; root has unrestricted access to the entire account.","D":"A time-limited password still creates a persistent IAM user with long-term access keys that aren't automatically disabled; the role assumption pattern is cleaner and more auditable."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_create_for-user.html","tags":["iam","cross-account","roles","third-party","audit"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a7b8c9d0","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM roles vs users: when to use each","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company has a CI/CD pipeline running on-premises that needs to deploy to AWS. Which credential approach is most secure?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM user with deployment permissions and store long-term access keys as CI/CD secrets","B":"Use IAM Roles Anywhere to issue short-term credentials to the on-premises workload using a trusted certificate authority","C":"Generate IAM user access keys and rotate them every 90 days manually","D":"Use the root account with programmatic access enabled"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM Roles Anywhere allows on-premises workloads to obtain temporary AWS credentials by authenticating with an X.509 certificate from a trusted PKI. This extends IAM roles to environments outside AWS without requiring long-term access keys. The temporary credentials are short-lived and automatically expire, following the same principle as EC2 instance profiles.","distractors":{"A":"Long-term access keys stored as CI/CD secrets are a common attack vector; if the CI/CD system is compromised, the keys remain valid until manually rotated.","C":"Manual key rotation every 90 days still means long-lived credentials that are exposed if the CI/CD system is breached; automatic short-term credentials are significantly more secure.","D":"Root programmatic access is a critical security violation; root credentials must never be used for programmatic access."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/rolesanywhere/latest/userguide/introduction.html","tags":["iam","roles-anywhere","cicd","on-premises","temporary-credentials"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b8c9d0e1","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM roles vs users: when to use each","difficulty":"hard","question":"A developer accidentally committed AWS IAM user access keys to a public GitHub repository. The keys are 3 minutes old. What are the FIRST two steps to take?","options":{"A":"Delete the IAM user, then rotate the keys","B":"Immediately deactivate or delete the access keys, then check CloudTrail for any API calls made with those keys in the past 3 minutes","C":"Change the IAM user's password, then enable MFA","D":"Enable AWS Config to detect future key exposures, then rotate the keys"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The immediate priority is to stop the exposed keys from being used: deactivate or delete them within seconds of discovery. Then, investigate CloudTrail to determine whether the keys were already used — GitHub public repos are scanned by automated bots within seconds of commit. Changing passwords is irrelevant to access key exposure, and enabling Config is a longer-term preventive measure, not an incident response action.","distractors":{"A":"Deleting the user is more disruptive than necessary and may disrupt legitimate workloads depending on the user; deactivating the specific keys is the targeted action. CloudTrail review must happen in parallel.","C":"Passwords are for console access; access keys are used for API access. Changing the password does not invalidate the exposed access keys.","D":"AWS Config is for configuration compliance monitoring; it does not revoke or deactivate exposed credentials. Delaying key deactivation while enabling Config allows continued exploitation."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_access-keys.html","tags":["iam","security-incident","access-keys","cloudtrail"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c9d0e1f2","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM roles vs users: when to use each","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement best describes when to use IAM roles instead of IAM users?","options":{"A":"Use roles for humans; use users for applications and services","B":"Use roles for AWS services, applications, cross-account access, and federated identities; use users only when a person needs long-term AWS console or programmatic access","C":"Roles and users are interchangeable; choose based on preference","D":"Use users for production workloads; use roles only for development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM roles should be used wherever temporary credentials are appropriate: AWS services (EC2, Lambda), applications, cross-account access, and federated identity (SSO, OIDC). IAM users with long-term credentials should be minimized — today, even human access is best handled via IAM Identity Center (SSO) which issues temporary credentials rather than long-term IAM user keys.","distractors":{"A":"This is backwards; AWS recommends roles for services and applications because they use short-term credentials, and SSO/federation for humans rather than IAM users.","C":"They are not interchangeable — roles provide temporary credentials with no storage required, which is fundamentally more secure than long-term user credentials.","D":"This has no basis in AWS security best practices; roles are actually recommended for production precisely because of their security properties."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id.html","tags":["iam","roles","users","best-practices"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d0e1f2a3","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"STS AssumeRole and cross-account access","difficulty":"medium","question":"An application in Account A needs to access resources in Account B. The trust policy on the role in Account B specifies Account A's root ARN. What additional step does an IAM entity in Account A need to assume the role?","options":{"A":"No additional step — the trust policy granting Account A root is sufficient for any principal in Account A","B":"The IAM entity in Account A must have an identity-based policy allowing sts:AssumeRole on the role ARN in Account B","C":"Account A must add Account B to its list of trusted accounts in the AWS console","D":"An AWS Support ticket must be opened to enable cross-account access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cross-account role assumption requires permissions on both sides: the trust policy on the role in Account B grants permission (who can assume), and the identity-based policy in Account A must include sts:AssumeRole on the role's ARN. When Account A's root ARN is in the trust policy, any principal in Account A CAN be granted assume-role permission, but each IAM entity in Account A still needs its own identity-based policy to actually assume it.","distractors":{"A":"Granting Account A's root ARN in the trust policy enables principals in Account A to be allowed, but each entity still needs an identity-based policy with sts:AssumeRole — the trust policy alone is not sufficient for sub-root principals.","C":"There is no 'trusted accounts' list in the AWS console separate from IAM trust policies; cross-account access is entirely managed via IAM trust policies and identity-based policies.","D":"Cross-account IAM access is fully self-service and requires no AWS Support involvement."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/tutorial_cross-account-with-roles.html","tags":["iam","sts","assume-role","cross-account","trust-policy"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e1f2a3b4","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"STS AssumeRole and cross-account access","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company wants to prevent credential exfiltration by ensuring that temporary credentials issued by STS for a role can only be used from within the company VPC. Which condition key enforces this?","options":{"A":"aws:SourceIp in the trust policy of the role","B":"aws:SourceVpc in a Deny statement in the role's permission policy or session policy","C":"aws:SourceVpce in the STS endpoint policy","D":"aws:PrincipalOrgID in the trust policy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adding a Deny statement with the aws:SourceVpc condition key (checking that requests originate from the specified VPC) in either the role's permission policy or a session policy ensures that even if temporary credentials are stolen, they cannot be used outside the VPC. The Deny fires for any request where aws:SourceVpc does not match, blocking use from external networks.","distractors":{"A":"aws:SourceIp in the trust policy controls who can assume the role, not where the issued credentials can be used. After assumption, SourceIp is not re-evaluated on subsequent API calls unless also in the permission policy.","C":"A VPC endpoint policy on the STS endpoint controls access to STS via that endpoint but doesn't restrict usage of already-issued credentials.","D":"aws:PrincipalOrgID restricts which AWS Organization members can assume the role; it does not enforce network-level constraints on credential usage."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_condition-keys.html","tags":["iam","sts","credentials","vpc","condition-key","exfiltration"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f2a3b4c5","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"STS AssumeRole and cross-account access","difficulty":"medium","question":"A web application uses Cognito to authenticate users. Authenticated users need time-limited access to specific S3 objects in their personal folder. Which STS feature enables this?","options":{"A":"STS AssumeRole with a session policy scoped to the user's folder prefix","B":"STS GetSessionToken to obtain temporary credentials for the S3 API","C":"IAM user with a time-limited access key per user","D":"S3 presigned URLs generated by a backend service for each user"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cognito identity pools can exchange Cognito tokens for temporary AWS credentials by assuming an IAM role via STS AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity. A session policy can be passed at assumption time to scope the credentials to just the user's specific S3 prefix (e.g., s3://bucket/user-id/*). This provides user-specific, temporary, least-privilege access without any per-user IAM configuration.","distractors":{"B":"GetSessionToken is used to create temporary credentials for an existing IAM user (typically for MFA enforcement); it doesn't easily map to per-Cognito-user scoping.","C":"Per-user IAM users with access keys are unscalable for web apps with thousands of users and violate the principle of avoiding long-term credentials.","D":"S3 presigned URLs are useful for specific objects but require a backend to generate each URL; they don't provide general S3 API access for client-side SDKs."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity.html","tags":["iam","sts","cognito","session-policy","s3"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a3b4c5d6","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"STS AssumeRole and cross-account access","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the maximum duration for temporary credentials issued by STS AssumeRole (for a standard IAM role)?","options":{"A":"1 hour","B":"12 hours","C":"24 hours","D":"The maximum is configurable up to 12 hours if the role's MaxSessionDuration allows it; default maximum is 1 hour"},"correct":"D","explanation":"STS AssumeRole issues temporary credentials with a duration you specify when calling the API. The minimum is 15 minutes, and the maximum is determined by the IAM role's MaxSessionDuration setting, which can be configured to a maximum of 12 hours (43200 seconds). The default MaxSessionDuration is 1 hour.","distractors":{"A":"1 hour is the default maximum but not the absolute limit; the role can be configured for up to 12 hours via MaxSessionDuration.","B":"12 hours is the absolute maximum when MaxSessionDuration is set to 43200 seconds, but it is not automatically available without configuring the role.","C":"24 hours exceeds the maximum allowed duration for AssumeRole (12 hours); some other credential types (like Cognito identity pool credentials) may have different durations."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.html","tags":["iam","sts","assume-role","session-duration"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b4c5d6e7","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations","difficulty":"medium","question":"An AWS Organization has an SCP on an OU that denies ec2:TerminateInstances. An IAM administrator in a member account has a policy that allows ec2:TerminateInstances. Can the administrator terminate EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Yes — IAM administrator permissions override Organization SCPs","B":"No — SCPs act as a guardrail that restricts even IAM administrators; the effective permission is the intersection of SCP and IAM policy","C":"Yes — SCPs only apply to non-administrator IAM roles","D":"No — SCPs override all IAM policies including those with AdministratorAccess"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SCPs define the maximum permissions available to accounts in an OU. The effective permissions for any principal equal the intersection of SCP allowances and IAM policy allowances. Even if an IAM policy grants ec2:TerminateInstances, the SCP Deny means the permission is not available. SCPs do NOT apply to the management account itself, but they do apply to all member accounts including those with administrator IAM users.","distractors":{"A":"IAM administrator permissions do not override SCPs; SCPs are a ceiling that applies above IAM policies.","C":"SCPs apply to all IAM principals in member accounts including administrators; there is no exemption for administrator-level IAM policies.","D":"The statement is correct that SCPs override IAM policies, but the explanation matters: SCPs define the maximum; they don't 'override' in the sense of replacing — they restrict the ceiling of what IAM policies can grant."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html","tags":["organizations","scp","guardrail","permissions-boundary"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c5d6e7f8","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company wants to enforce that no IAM user or role in any member account can disable AWS CloudTrail. They also want to allow their security team (in the management account) to manage CloudTrail. How should SCPs be configured?","options":{"A":"Attach an SCP to all OUs denying cloudtrail:StopLogging and cloudtrail:DeleteTrail; SCPs do not apply to the management account","B":"Attach an SCP to the root of the Organization denying cloudtrail:StopLogging for all principals","C":"Attach an SCP to member accounts only, using a condition to exclude the security team's ARN","D":"Enable CloudTrail organization trails from the management account — no SCP needed"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SCPs attached to OUs (not the management account itself) prevent member accounts from disabling CloudTrail. SCPs do not apply to the management account by design, so the security team in the management account retains full control. Attaching the SCP to all OUs containing member accounts achieves the desired restriction without blocking the security team.","distractors":{"B":"Attaching to the Organization root would seem to apply to all accounts, but SCPs still never apply to the management account — the security team in the management account is unaffected regardless. This option works but attaching to OUs is more precise.","C":"SCPs cannot use principal ARN conditions to exclude specific users; they apply based on organizational structure (OU membership), not individual principal exemptions.","D":"An organization trail ensures the trail exists and is visible in all accounts, but it does not prevent member account principals from attempting to disable or delete other trails; an SCP is still required to block the action."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html","tags":["organizations","scp","cloudtrail","management-account"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d6e7f8a9","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company wants to restrict all member accounts to only deploy resources in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. Which SCP approach implements this most cleanly?","options":{"A":"Attach a Deny SCP that blocks all actions where aws:RequestedRegion is not in [us-east-1, eu-west-1]","B":"Create separate Allow SCPs for us-east-1 and eu-west-1 and attach both to the root","C":"Attach a Deny SCP that blocks ec2:RunInstances, rds:CreateDBInstance, and s3:CreateBucket in non-approved regions","D":"Use AWS Config rules to detect and remediate resources deployed in non-approved regions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An SCP using a Deny statement with a NotAction (excluding global services) and a Condition on aws:RequestedRegion blocks all actions in unapproved regions in a single, maintainable policy. This covers all services without enumerating them. Global services like IAM and STS that don't have a regional endpoint must be excluded via NotAction to avoid breaking the account.","distractors":{"B":"SCPs work as an 'allow list' only if the FullAWSAccess policy is removed; otherwise, adding Allow SCPs without removing FullAWSAccess has no restrictive effect since the default is allow-all.","C":"Enumerating every regional service action is unmaintainable — AWS adds new services constantly, and this approach would miss them; the aws:RequestedRegion condition approach covers all services.","D":"AWS Config detects and can remediate but operates reactively; resources are created first, then detected and removed. SCPs proactively prevent creation."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps_examples_general.html","tags":["organizations","scp","region-restriction","aws:RequestedRegion"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e7f8a9b0","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Service Control Policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which AWS account is NOT affected by SCPs?","options":{"A":"Member accounts in OUs with SCPs attached","B":"The AWS Organizations management (master) account","C":"Accounts in the root of the organization","D":"Accounts with AdministratorAccess IAM policies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SCPs never apply to the management account of an AWS Organization. The management account has full access to all services regardless of any SCPs. This is by design — the management account needs unrestricted access to manage the organization itself. This is why the management account should be used exclusively for organization management and not for workloads.","distractors":{"A":"Member accounts in OUs are fully subject to any SCPs attached to their OU or parent OUs — this is the primary purpose of SCPs.","C":"Accounts directly under the organization root are subject to SCPs attached at the root level (except the management account itself).","D":"AdministratorAccess is an IAM policy and provides no exemption from SCPs; SCPs constrain all IAM principals in member accounts."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html","tags":["organizations","scp","management-account"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f8a9b0c1","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC security groups vs NACLs: key differences","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which statement correctly describes a key difference between VPC Security Groups and Network Access Control Lists (NACLs)?","options":{"A":"Security groups are stateless; NACLs are stateful","B":"Security groups are stateful; NACLs are stateless","C":"Security groups apply at the subnet level; NACLs apply at the instance level","D":"Both security groups and NACLs are stateful"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Security groups are stateful: if inbound traffic is allowed, the return traffic is automatically permitted regardless of outbound rules. NACLs are stateless: both inbound and outbound rules must explicitly allow traffic in both directions, including ephemeral ports for return traffic. This is one of the most tested distinctions on the SAA-C03 exam.","distractors":{"A":"This is backwards — security groups are stateful, NACLs are stateless.","C":"Security groups apply at the ENI (instance/resource) level; NACLs apply at the subnet level. The statement has them reversed.","D":"NACLs are explicitly stateless; both cannot be stateful."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html","tags":["vpc","security-groups","nacl","stateful","stateless"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a9b0c1d2","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC security groups vs NACLs: key differences","difficulty":"medium","question":"A web server in a public subnet allows inbound HTTP on port 80 via its security group. A NACL on the public subnet allows inbound traffic on port 80. Users are unable to reach the server. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"The security group is missing an outbound rule allowing HTTP","B":"The NACL is missing an outbound rule allowing ephemeral ports (1024-65535) for return traffic","C":"The security group needs an explicit Allow for the return traffic","D":"The Internet Gateway is not attached to the VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NACLs are stateless, meaning return traffic must be explicitly allowed. When a client browser connects to port 80, the server responds on the ephemeral (high) port that the client chose (typically 1024-65535). The NACL must have an outbound rule allowing traffic on those ephemeral ports for the TCP connection to complete. The security group's stateful nature means this is not needed at the security group level.","distractors":{"A":"Security groups are stateful; if inbound is allowed, return traffic is automatically allowed outbound — no explicit outbound rule is needed for return traffic in a security group.","C":"Security groups never need explicit rules for return traffic due to their stateful nature; this is only a NACL concern.","D":"If the Internet Gateway were missing, clients couldn't reach the server at all (no route), but the question describes a more specific connectivity issue with NACLs already partially configured."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-network-acls.html","tags":["vpc","nacl","stateless","ephemeral-ports","security-groups"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b0c1d2e3","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC security groups vs NACLs: key differences","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company needs to block a specific malicious IP address from accessing any resources in a VPC subnet immediately. Security groups on 50 different EC2 instances each allow all traffic. What is the fastest single-configuration fix?","options":{"A":"Add a Deny rule to each of the 50 security groups blocking the IP","B":"Add a DENY rule with the lowest rule number in the subnet's NACL blocking the malicious IP","C":"Add the IP to an IAM policy condition denying access","D":"Block the IP using AWS WAF on the Application Load Balancer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NACLs support explicit Deny rules and apply to all resources in a subnet. Adding a single Deny rule at the subnet NACL level blocks the IP from reaching all 50 instances immediately without touching any security groups. Security groups do not support Deny rules — they are allow-only. NACL rules are evaluated in ascending order, so a low rule number ensures the Deny is evaluated first.","distractors":{"A":"Security groups only support Allow rules — there is no Deny rule in a security group. You can only remove the rules that allow the IP, which doesn't work when 'all traffic' is allowed.","C":"IAM policies control AWS API access, not VPC network traffic; an IAM Deny would not block network-level connections to EC2 instances.","D":"WAF only protects resources behind ALB; it does not block network-level traffic to EC2 instances that receive direct connections, and requires WAF to already be configured."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-network-acls.html","tags":["vpc","nacl","deny","security-groups","ip-blocking"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c1d2e3f4","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC security groups vs NACLs: key differences","difficulty":"medium","question":"Two EC2 instances (A and B) are in the same security group and the same subnet. The security group allows all inbound traffic from the internet. Can instance A communicate with instance B?","options":{"A":"Yes — instances in the same security group can always communicate with each other","B":"Only if the security group has a rule allowing inbound traffic from the security group itself","C":"Yes — same-subnet instances can always communicate regardless of security groups","D":"No — security groups block all intra-VPC traffic by default"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Security groups do NOT automatically allow communication between instances in the same group. For instances in the same security group to communicate, you need a security group rule that references the security group itself as a source. A common rule allows all traffic from the security group's own ID, enabling intra-group communication. Without this self-referencing rule, instances in the same group follow the same allow/deny logic as any other connection.","distractors":{"A":"Membership in the same security group alone does not grant inter-instance access; a self-referencing rule is required.","C":"Subnet boundaries are controlled by NACLs, not security groups; being in the same subnet doesn't automatically allow communication without the appropriate security group rule.","D":"Security groups do not block all intra-VPC traffic by default — they allow whatever rules are configured; the default security group actually does allow intra-group traffic, but a custom security group without a self-referencing rule does not."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html","tags":["vpc","security-groups","intra-group","self-referencing"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d2e3f4a5","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC endpoints: Gateway vs Interface","difficulty":"medium","question":"An EC2 instance in a private subnet needs to access Amazon S3 without routing traffic through the internet or a NAT Gateway. Which VPC endpoint type should be used?","options":{"A":"Interface VPC endpoint for S3 using AWS PrivateLink","B":"Gateway VPC endpoint for S3 added to the route table","C":"NAT Gateway in the public subnet routing to S3","D":"VPN connection to an S3 regional endpoint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"S3 and DynamoDB support Gateway VPC endpoints, which are free and work by adding an entry to the VPC route table. Traffic to S3 is routed through the gateway endpoint rather than the internet, staying on the AWS private network. No NAT Gateway is needed, saving cost. Gateway endpoints are always the preferred choice for S3 and DynamoDB due to their simplicity and zero additional cost.","distractors":{"A":"An Interface VPC endpoint for S3 (using PrivateLink) exists but is more expensive than the Gateway endpoint (it incurs hourly ENI charges and data processing fees); the Gateway endpoint is preferred for S3 in most scenarios.","C":"A NAT Gateway would route S3 traffic through the internet gateway and back, incurring NAT Gateway data processing fees unnecessarily; the VPC endpoint keeps traffic on the AWS backbone.","D":"VPN connects on-premises networks to AWS; it is not used for private EC2-to-S3 connectivity within a VPC."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html","tags":["vpc-endpoint","gateway-endpoint","s3","private-subnet"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e3f4a5b6","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC endpoints: Gateway vs Interface","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company needs on-premises servers to access AWS Systems Manager (SSM) endpoints privately without traversing the public internet, using their existing Direct Connect connection. Which endpoint type enables this?","options":{"A":"Gateway VPC endpoint for SSM added to the on-premises route table","B":"Interface VPC endpoint for SSM in the VPC, accessible from on-premises via Direct Connect","C":"Gateway VPC endpoint for SSM — accessible from on-premises via Direct Connect","D":"AWS PrivateLink service endpoint connected directly to the on-premises router"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Interface VPC endpoints create ENIs with private IP addresses within the VPC. Since Direct Connect connects on-premises to the VPC's private address space, on-premises servers can reach the ENI's private IP of the Interface endpoint, thus accessing SSM privately. Gateway endpoints only modify route tables within the VPC and are not accessible from outside the VPC (including on-premises via Direct Connect or VPN).","distractors":{"A":"Gateway endpoints cannot be added to on-premises route tables; they work by adding routes to VPC route tables. On-premises connectivity is not supported by Gateway endpoints.","C":"Gateway endpoints are not accessible from on-premises via Direct Connect — only Interface endpoints can be reached from outside the VPC.","D":"AWS PrivateLink is the mechanism behind Interface VPC endpoints, not a separate product that connects directly to on-premises routers."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints.html","tags":["vpc-endpoint","interface-endpoint","direct-connect","on-premises","privatelink"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f4a5b6c7","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC endpoints: Gateway vs Interface","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which two AWS services support Gateway VPC endpoints (not Interface endpoints)?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon SQS and Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon EC2 and Amazon RDS","D":"AWS Lambda and Amazon Kinesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Only Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB support Gateway VPC endpoints. All other AWS services that support private VPC connectivity use Interface VPC endpoints (powered by AWS PrivateLink). Gateway endpoints are free and work via route table entries, while Interface endpoints incur ENI and data processing charges.","distractors":{"B":"SQS and SNS use Interface VPC endpoints, not Gateway endpoints.","C":"EC2 and RDS are services that run in a VPC by default; they are not accessed via VPC endpoints — they are the resources being accessed.","D":"Lambda and Kinesis use Interface VPC endpoints, not Gateway endpoints."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/gateway-endpoints.html","tags":["vpc-endpoint","gateway-endpoint","s3","dynamodb"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a5b6c7d8","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS PrivateLink","difficulty":"medium","question":"A SaaS vendor wants to expose their service to hundreds of customer AWS accounts without requiring VPC peering or public internet access. Customers should not need to manage complex routing. Which AWS feature enables this?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering between the vendor's VPC and each customer VPC","B":"AWS Transit Gateway with the vendor's VPC as a spoke","C":"AWS PrivateLink — the vendor creates a VPC endpoint service; customers create Interface VPC endpoints connecting to it","D":"AWS Direct Connect with a hosted connection to each customer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink allows a service provider to expose a service via a VPC endpoint service backed by a Network Load Balancer. Customers create Interface VPC endpoints in their VPCs that connect to the service privately using private IP addresses. This scales to thousands of customers without VPC CIDR conflicts or complex routing, and all traffic stays on the AWS backbone.","distractors":{"A":"VPC peering requires one peering connection per customer VPC, has CIDR conflict constraints, and does not scale to hundreds of customers cleanly.","B":"Transit Gateway is for network connectivity between VPCs within the same organization; it shares routing tables and is not appropriate for a multi-tenant service model.","D":"Direct Connect physical connections are expensive and take weeks to provision; it's not a scalable solution for exposing a SaaS service to hundreds of customers."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/privatelink-share-your-services.html","tags":["privatelink","vpc-endpoint-service","saas","nlb"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b6c7d8e9","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS PrivateLink","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company uses AWS PrivateLink to expose an internal API. The API is backed by an NLB, and customers connect via Interface VPC endpoints. A customer reports intermittent connection failures. What is the most likely cause to investigate first?","options":{"A":"The NLB health checks are failing on backend targets, causing the NLB to have no healthy targets in the AZ matching the customer's endpoint","B":"The PrivateLink service has reached its maximum number of connections","C":"The customer's VPC does not have DNS resolution enabled","D":"The Interface endpoint's security group is blocking TCP traffic on port 443"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink connects through NLB, and if NLB health checks fail for backend targets in a specific AZ, connections routed to that AZ will fail. This causes intermittent failures because the client may be assigned to a healthy or unhealthy AZ depending on DNS round-robin. Checking NLB target group health status is the first diagnostic step for PrivateLink connectivity issues.","distractors":{"B":"AWS PrivateLink does not have a published fixed limit on concurrent connections that would cause intermittent failures under normal usage; this would only appear under extreme load.","C":"DNS resolution issues would cause consistent failures (DNS not resolving) rather than intermittent connection failures; also, the customer may be using IP-based connectivity.","D":"Interface endpoint security groups could block traffic, but this would cause consistent failures, not intermittent ones (all connections would fail, not some)."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/troubleshoot-endpoint-service.html","tags":["privatelink","nlb","health-checks","troubleshooting"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c7d8e9f0","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"KMS key policies and key rotation","difficulty":"medium","question":"An administrator wants to ensure that a KMS CMK can only be used by specific IAM roles, not by the root account or any other IAM identity. How is this achieved?","options":{"A":"Remove the default key policy statement that grants the root account access, and add statements for only the specific IAM roles","B":"Create an IAM policy denying all other principals access to kms:Decrypt","C":"Enable automatic key rotation, which restricts key usage to the specified roles","D":"Set the KMS key's DeletionWindowInDays to lock the key to specific principals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"KMS key policies are the primary access control for CMKs. The default key policy includes a statement that enables IAM policies to control access. If you replace the key policy entirely with only statements for the specific IAM roles, you remove both root account access and the IAM policy delegation — meaning only the explicitly listed roles can use the key. This is the only way to restrict root account access to a KMS key.","distractors":{"B":"IAM deny policies can restrict access for specific IAM principals but cannot override the root account's access, which is established in the key policy itself. Key policy is the authoritative access control for KMS CMKs.","C":"Automatic key rotation rotates the cryptographic material of the key periodically for security; it has no effect on which principals can use the key.","D":"DeletionWindowInDays is for scheduling key deletion; it is unrelated to key access control."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/key-policy-overview.html","tags":["kms","key-policy","cmk","access-control"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d8e9f0a1","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"KMS key policies and key rotation","difficulty":"easy","question":"What happens to data encrypted with a KMS key when automatic key rotation is enabled?","options":{"A":"All existing encrypted data is automatically re-encrypted with the new key material","B":"New data is encrypted with the new key material; existing encrypted data remains encrypted under the old key material, which KMS retains for decryption","C":"The key is replaced entirely; existing data encrypted with the old key becomes permanently inaccessible","D":"Automatic rotation invalidates the CMK and a new CMK must be created"},"correct":"B","explanation":"KMS automatic key rotation creates new key material while retaining the old key material within the same KMS key (same key ID and ARN). New encryption operations use the new material. Existing data is not re-encrypted — KMS retains all previous key material versions and uses the correct version for decryption automatically. From the application's perspective, nothing changes.","distractors":{"A":"KMS does not automatically re-encrypt existing data; re-encryption must be triggered explicitly by the application if desired.","C":"Old key material is never discarded during rotation; it is retained for decryption of data previously encrypted with it. Existing data remains fully accessible.","D":"The key ID, ARN, alias, and all other identifiers remain the same; only the underlying cryptographic material changes."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/rotate-keys.html","tags":["kms","key-rotation","cryptography","backward-compatibility"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e9f0a1b2","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"KMS key policies and key rotation","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company shares encrypted EBS snapshots with another AWS account. The snapshots are encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key. What must be done for the recipient account to use the snapshot?","options":{"A":"Nothing — sharing an encrypted snapshot automatically grants KMS key access","B":"Share the snapshot with the recipient account AND update the KMS key policy to grant the recipient account kms:DescribeKey and kms:CreateGrant (or kms:Decrypt)","C":"Re-encrypt the snapshot with an AWS managed key before sharing","D":"Convert the snapshot to an unencrypted volume, create a new snapshot, and share it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"To use a shared encrypted EBS snapshot, the recipient account needs both: (1) the snapshot shared via EC2 sharing permissions, and (2) access to the KMS key used for encryption — granted via the key policy. Without KMS key access, the recipient cannot decrypt the snapshot to create a volume. Typically you grant the recipient kms:DescribeKey, kms:CreateGrant, and kms:Decrypt.","distractors":{"A":"Sharing the snapshot does not automatically grant KMS key access; the KMS key policy must be explicitly updated to include the recipient account.","C":"AWS managed keys cannot be shared across accounts; you would need to re-encrypt with a customer-managed key already shared with the recipient. The correct approach is sharing access to the CMK, not switching key types.","D":"Creating an unencrypted snapshot is a security anti-pattern; data would be exposed in transit. The correct approach preserves encryption."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ebs-modifying-snapshot-permissions.html","tags":["kms","ebs-snapshot","cross-account","key-policy","sharing"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f0a1b2c3","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Secrets Manager vs SSM Parameter Store","difficulty":"medium","question":"An application needs to store a database password that must be automatically rotated every 30 days. The rotation must trigger a Lambda function that updates the database. Which service is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store (SecureString)","B":"AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation configured and a Lambda rotation function","C":"AWS KMS with a custom key alias storing the password as metadata","D":"AWS Certificate Manager storing the password as a certificate attribute"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Secrets Manager is designed for secrets that require automatic rotation. It has native integration with Lambda rotation functions and built-in templates for common databases (RDS, Redshift, DocumentDB). When rotation triggers, Secrets Manager calls the Lambda function which updates the secret and the database simultaneously. Parameter Store supports encryption but does not have built-in automatic rotation.","distractors":{"A":"SSM Parameter Store SecureString stores encrypted parameters but does not have built-in automatic rotation; you would need to build and schedule a custom rotation mechanism yourself.","C":"KMS is an encryption key service, not a secrets storage service; it does not store application secrets or provide a rotation mechanism.","D":"ACM manages TLS/SSL certificates for use with AWS services; it is not a general-purpose secrets store."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html","tags":["secrets-manager","parameter-store","rotation","lambda","database"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a1b2c3e5","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Secrets Manager vs SSM Parameter Store","difficulty":"medium","question":"A startup needs to store thousands of application configuration values, most of which are non-sensitive (feature flags, timeouts), with a small number of sensitive API keys. They want the lowest cost solution. What is the recommended approach?","options":{"A":"Store all values in Secrets Manager for simplicity","B":"Store non-sensitive values in SSM Parameter Store standard parameters; store sensitive API keys in Secrets Manager","C":"Store all values in SSM Parameter Store SecureString (KMS-encrypted)","D":"Store all values in DynamoDB with server-side encryption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SSM Parameter Store standard parameters are free for non-sensitive configuration values (feature flags, timeouts). Secrets Manager is purpose-built for sensitive secrets requiring rotation and has per-secret pricing. Combining the two minimizes cost: free Parameter Store for bulk configuration, Secrets Manager only for secrets requiring rotation and advanced management.","distractors":{"A":"Storing all values in Secrets Manager is expensive at scale — each secret incurs per-secret and per-API-call charges; most feature flags and configuration don't need Secrets Manager's features.","C":"All SSM Parameter Store SecureString values use KMS, incurring KMS API charges for every parameter access; this is fine for sensitive values but adds unnecessary cost for thousands of non-sensitive parameters.","D":"DynamoDB is not a secrets or configuration management service; using it for this purpose adds operational complexity without the security features of Parameter Store or Secrets Manager."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/systems-manager-parameter-store.html","tags":["secrets-manager","parameter-store","cost","configuration-management"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b2c3d4f6","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Secrets Manager vs SSM Parameter Store","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which feature is available in AWS Secrets Manager but NOT in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store?","options":{"A":"Encryption with AWS KMS","B":"Native automatic rotation with built-in Lambda templates for AWS databases","C":"Cross-region replication of secrets","D":"IAM policy-based access control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Secrets Manager has native automatic rotation built in, with pre-built Lambda rotation function templates for RDS MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server, Redshift, and DocumentDB. It handles the rotation lifecycle entirely. Parameter Store does not have a built-in rotation mechanism — you must build and schedule your own rotation solution.","distractors":{"A":"Both Secrets Manager and Parameter Store (SecureString) support encryption with AWS KMS; this is not a differentiator.","C":"Secrets Manager does support cross-region replication, but this is a premium feature that Parameter Store also now partially supports; it's not the primary differentiating capability.","D":"Both services use IAM policies for access control; this is not a differentiator."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html","tags":["secrets-manager","parameter-store","rotation","comparison"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c3d4e5g7","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company wants to enable HTTPS on their Application Load Balancer using a TLS certificate for their domain. Which service provides free, automatically renewing TLS certificates for use with ALB?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS — generate a TLS certificate using a customer-managed key","B":"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) — provision a public certificate at no cost for use with supported AWS services","C":"AWS IAM — upload a certificate to the IAM certificate store","D":"AWS Secrets Manager — store the certificate and private key, reference from ALB"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACM provides free public TLS certificates that integrate directly with ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway, and other AWS services. ACM automatically renews certificates before they expire, eliminating manual renewal processes. The certificate private key never leaves AWS; ACM handles the cryptographic operations on behalf of the ALB.","distractors":{"A":"KMS is an encryption key service that generates symmetric and asymmetric keys for use with AWS services; it does not provision TLS certificates for domain validation.","C":"IAM certificate store is a legacy feature for uploading third-party certificates to use with older services; ACM is the modern, recommended approach for new deployments.","D":"Secrets Manager can store certificate material but does not validate domains, issue certificates, or integrate directly with ALB the way ACM does."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/acm-overview.html","tags":["acm","tls","certificates","alb","https"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d4e5f6h8","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company needs to use a TLS certificate issued by ACM on an EC2 instance running NGINX. Can they export the private key from ACM?","options":{"A":"Yes — ACM allows private key export for all certificate types","B":"No — ACM-provisioned public certificates cannot be exported; only private certificates from ACM Private CA can be exported","C":"Yes — with root account permissions you can export any ACM certificate","D":"No — ACM certificates can only be used with Route 53 DNS records"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACM-provisioned public certificates (domain-validated, free) cannot be exported; their private keys never leave AWS. ACM is designed for use with integrated AWS services (ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway). For EC2 instances running their own TLS termination (NGINX, Apache), you must either use ACM Private CA (which supports export) or obtain a certificate from an external CA and install it manually.","distractors":{"A":"ACM public certificates do not allow private key export regardless of account permissions; this is a deliberate security design.","C":"Root account permissions do not grant the ability to export ACM public certificate private keys; the private key is managed entirely by AWS and is not accessible.","D":"ACM integrates with many services beyond Route 53; it works with ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway, and Elastic Beanstalk, among others."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/acm-certificate.html","tags":["acm","private-key","export","ec2","private-ca"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e5f6a7c9","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS WAF and Shield: DDoS layers","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's web application hosted on CloudFront is experiencing a layer 7 HTTP flood attack where attackers send millions of requests with valid-looking User-Agent headers. Which AWS service mitigates this attack?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield Standard — automatically mitigates all DDoS attacks","B":"AWS WAF with rate-based rules that limit requests per IP, deployed on the CloudFront distribution","C":"AWS Shield Advanced — provides automatic application layer protection","D":"Amazon GuardDuty — detects and blocks the malicious IP addresses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS WAF is the correct tool for Layer 7 (application layer) HTTP flood attacks. Rate-based rules in WAF automatically block IP addresses that exceed a configurable requests-per-5-minutes threshold. WAF can inspect HTTP headers, URIs, and bodies to create sophisticated allow/deny rules. Deployed on CloudFront, it protects at the edge before traffic reaches the origin.","distractors":{"A":"Shield Standard protects against common network and transport layer DDoS attacks (Layers 3/4) such as SYN floods and UDP reflection attacks; it does not provide application layer (Layer 7) rate limiting.","C":"Shield Advanced provides enhanced DDoS protection and cost protection, and includes WAF at no extra charge for protected resources; however, WAF rules must still be configured. Shield Advanced alone without WAF rate-based rules does not stop HTTP floods.","D":"GuardDuty is a threat detection service that generates findings; it does not actively block traffic. It has no mechanism to block individual IPs from accessing CloudFront."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-rate-based-rules.html","tags":["waf","ddos","rate-based-rules","cloudfront","layer7"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f6a7b8d0","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS WAF and Shield: DDoS layers","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which AWS service protects against network-level DDoS attacks (Layer 3/4) such as SYN floods at no additional cost for all AWS customers?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS Shield Standard","C":"AWS Shield Advanced","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Shield Standard is automatically enabled for all AWS customers at no additional cost. It protects against the most common and frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks (Layers 3 and 4), including SYN floods, UDP floods, and reflection attacks. Shield Standard is always active for AWS resources that have an internet-facing entry point.","distractors":{"A":"AWS WAF is a web application firewall that operates at Layer 7 (application layer) and requires configuration; it is not free (charges based on rules and requests).","C":"AWS Shield Advanced is a premium service with additional DDoS protection, dedicated support, and cost protection; it has a significant monthly fee.","D":"Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability assessment service for EC2 instances and container images; it does not provide DDoS protection."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/shield-chapter.html","tags":["shield","ddos","layer3","layer4","free"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a7b8c9e1","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"AWS WAF and Shield: DDoS layers","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company uses Shield Advanced and experiences a DDoS attack that causes increased AWS costs (CloudFront data transfer, EC2 usage). What Shield Advanced feature addresses this?","options":{"A":"Shield Advanced automatically caps usage during attacks to limit costs","B":"Shield Advanced provides DDoS cost protection — credits for scaling charges caused by DDoS attacks that Shield failed to mitigate","C":"Shield Advanced refunds the full monthly bill when any DDoS attack occurs","D":"Shield Advanced routes attack traffic to a scrubbing center and only charges for clean traffic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Shield Advanced includes DDoS cost protection, which provides service credits for scaling-related charges that result from a DDoS attack. If a DDoS attack causes your CloudFront, Route 53, or EC2 usage to spike, you can request credits for those charges through the AWS Support case associated with the attack event. The attack must be detected and confirmed by AWS Shield Advanced.","distractors":{"A":"Shield Advanced does not cap usage; capping usage during an attack would mean the application becomes unavailable, defeating the purpose of DDoS protection.","C":"Shield Advanced does not refund the full monthly bill; it provides targeted credits for DDoS-caused scaling charges through a formal request process.","D":"While AWS does route attack traffic through scrubbing infrastructure, Shield Advanced still charges for clean traffic; the cost protection feature covers excess scaling costs, not all traffic charges."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/ddos-manage-subscriptions-shield-advanced.html","tags":["shield-advanced","ddos","cost-protection","credits"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b8c9d0f2","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Amazon Cognito: user pools vs identity pools","difficulty":"medium","question":"A mobile app needs to allow users to sign up and sign in with their email/password, and also via Google and Facebook. After authentication, users need AWS credentials to call an S3 API directly from their device. Which Cognito components are needed?","options":{"A":"Cognito User Pool only — it provides both authentication and AWS credentials","B":"Cognito User Pool for authentication and sign-in; Cognito Identity Pool to exchange the user pool token for temporary AWS credentials","C":"Cognito Identity Pool only — it handles both social login and AWS credential vending","D":"Cognito User Pool for authentication; IAM users created per app user for AWS access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cognito User Pool is the identity directory that handles user registration, sign-in, MFA, and federation with social providers. It issues JWT tokens (ID/access/refresh). Cognito Identity Pool takes those tokens (or social provider tokens directly) and exchanges them for temporary AWS credentials via STS, enabling direct AWS API calls from the device. Both components serve different roles.","distractors":{"A":"Cognito User Pools issue JWT tokens for application authentication but do not issue AWS IAM credentials; that is the role of the Identity Pool.","C":"Identity Pools can accept social provider tokens directly, but for a custom user directory with email/password, a User Pool is required to manage those identities first.","D":"Creating IAM users per app user is completely unscalable for a mobile app and violates the principle of using roles for applications."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-identity-pools.html","tags":["cognito","user-pool","identity-pool","federation","aws-credentials"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c9d0e1g3","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Amazon Cognito: user pools vs identity pools","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the primary purpose of an Amazon Cognito User Pool?","options":{"A":"To provide temporary AWS credentials to mobile and web app users","B":"To manage user registration, authentication, and issue JWT tokens for applications","C":"To federate users from corporate Active Directory into AWS IAM roles","D":"To store user preference data such as app settings and themes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Cognito User Pool is a user directory that provides sign-up, sign-in, MFA, account recovery, and social identity federation (Google, Facebook, Apple, SAML). After authentication, it issues JWT tokens (ID token, access token, refresh token) for use by the application. It is focused on application-level user management, not AWS resource access.","distractors":{"A":"Temporary AWS credentials are issued by Cognito Identity Pools (via STS), not User Pools. User Pools issue application-level JWT tokens.","C":"While User Pools can federate SAML/OIDC providers (including Active Directory via ADFS), the primary purpose is user management and authentication, not IAM federation specifically.","D":"User Pools store user profile attributes but are not a general-purpose preference store; DynamoDB or other databases are used for application preference storage."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-user-identity-pools.html","tags":["cognito","user-pool","authentication","jwt"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d0e1f2h4","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Amazon Inspector and GuardDuty: when to use each","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which AWS service continuously monitors your AWS accounts for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior using ML and threat intelligence?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty is a continuous threat detection service that analyzes CloudTrail events, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs using machine learning and threat intelligence to identify malicious activity (compromised EC2 instances, cryptocurrency mining, unusual API calls, etc.). It requires no agent installation and activates with a single click.","distractors":{"A":"Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure; it identifies what could be exploited, not active threats.","C":"AWS Config tracks resource configuration changes and evaluates compliance against defined rules; it is not a threat detection service.","D":"AWS Security Hub aggregates findings from multiple security services (including GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie) into a unified dashboard; it is an aggregator, not a detection engine."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/what-is-guardduty.html","tags":["guardduty","threat-detection","security-monitoring"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-e1f2a3i5","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Amazon Inspector and GuardDuty: when to use each","difficulty":"medium","question":"A security team wants to identify EC2 instances with unpatched software vulnerabilities (CVEs) and open network ports that are accessible from the internet. Which service provides this?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty — scans EC2 for CVEs and open ports","B":"Amazon Inspector — performs continuous vulnerability scanning of EC2 instances and container images","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor — checks for security vulnerabilities in EC2 instances","D":"Amazon Macie — scans EC2 instances for sensitive data and vulnerabilities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Inspector automatically discovers and scans EC2 instances (via SSM agent) and ECR container images for software vulnerabilities (CVE database) and unintended network reachability (open ports accessible from the internet). It continuously rescans when new CVEs are published or when software packages are updated on the instance.","distractors":{"A":"GuardDuty detects active threats and anomalous behavior in real time; it does not scan for unpatched software or known CVEs on EC2 instances.","C":"Trusted Advisor provides best-practice recommendations across security, cost, and performance; its security checks are high-level (MFA on root, open security groups) and do not perform CVE-level vulnerability scanning.","D":"Amazon Macie is a data security service that uses ML to discover and classify sensitive data (PII, credentials) in S3; it does not scan EC2 instances for vulnerabilities."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/inspector/latest/user/findings-understanding.html","tags":["inspector","vulnerability","cve","ec2","network-reachability"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-f2a3b4j6","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Amazon Inspector and GuardDuty: when to use each","difficulty":"hard","question":"GuardDuty generates a finding: 'UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/MaliciousIPCaller'. An IAM user is making API calls from a known malicious IP. What are the FIRST two response actions?","options":{"A":"Delete the IAM user immediately, then open an AWS Support case","B":"Disable the IAM user's access keys and attach an explicit Deny policy to the user; then investigate CloudTrail for the scope of actions taken","C":"Enable MFA on the IAM user, then rotate all access keys in the account","D":"Block the IP in a NACL, then check GuardDuty for additional findings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The immediate priority is to contain the compromise by deactivating the exposed credentials: disable the access keys and attach an explicit Deny policy as belt-and-suspenders. Then investigate CloudTrail to understand what APIs were called with those keys and assess the blast radius. Deleting the user is more disruptive than necessary and could destroy forensic information about the compromise.","distractors":{"A":"Deleting the IAM user destroys audit trail information about the user's configuration and may cause service disruptions; disabling keys and adding a Deny policy is the targeted containment action.","C":"Enabling MFA on a compromised user doesn't help if the attacker already has long-term access keys (MFA is for console access, not API key usage). Rotating all account keys is too broad and disruptive.","D":"Blocking the IP in a NACL stops traffic from that IP to VPC resources but does not revoke the IAM user's access keys, which could be used from any IP. The primary containment action is credential revocation."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/guardduty_remediate.html","tags":["guardduty","incident-response","iam","access-keys","cloudtrail"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-a3b4c5k7","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"S3 bucket policies, ACLs, and Object Lock","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company wants to make objects in their S3 bucket publicly readable (for a static website) without enabling S3 public access block settings. Which approach is correct?","options":{"A":"Set the bucket ACL to public-read","B":"Add a bucket policy with an Allow for s3:GetObject for Principal: '*', and ensure S3 Block Public Access settings at the bucket and account level allow public policies","C":"Enable S3 Static Website Hosting — this automatically makes objects public","D":"Remove all bucket policies and ACLs — the default is public access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For public S3 static websites, the standard approach is a bucket policy allowing s3:GetObject for all principals ('*'). However, this only works if the S3 Block Public Access settings (at both bucket and account levels) are turned off for public policies. Simply enabling static website hosting does not make objects public; access control must be explicitly configured.","distractors":{"A":"S3 ACLs are being deprecated and AWS recommends disabling ACLs and using bucket policies instead. Additionally, ACL public-read on the bucket does not affect object-level access without also setting object ACLs.","C":"Static website hosting enables the website endpoint and sets error/index documents; it does not change the access control of any objects.","D":"S3 buckets are private by default; removing policies and ACLs results in objects being inaccessible (implicit deny), not public."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/WebsiteAccessPermissionsReqd.html","tags":["s3","bucket-policy","public-access","static-website"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-b4c5d6l8","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"S3 bucket policies, ACLs, and Object Lock","difficulty":"hard","question":"A security audit reveals that a developer in Account A can access sensitive objects in an S3 bucket in Account B using their IAM role. The bucket policy in Account B only allows Account B's root access. The developer's role in Account A has no explicit S3 policies. How is this access possible?","options":{"A":"The developer's IAM role has AdministratorAccess which grants cross-account S3 access automatically","B":"S3 access is public — the Block Public Access settings are not enabled in Account B","C":"This is not possible with the described configuration; the scenario is incorrect","D":"A bucket ACL grants public-read or the specific role access, bypassing the bucket policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"S3 ACLs are evaluated in addition to bucket policies. If an ACL on the bucket or individual objects grants access (e.g., public-read or a specific account/canonical ID), that access is permitted even if the bucket policy only allows Account B. ACLs and bucket policies both apply, and access is granted if either one allows it (for same-account) or both agree (for cross-account). Auditing must check both bucket policies AND ACLs.","distractors":{"A":"AdministratorAccess grants full IAM permissions within the account but does not automatically grant cross-account resource access; cross-account access requires explicit permission on the resource side.","B":"If Block Public Access is disabled and the bucket is public, any IAM principal can read objects — this is a plausible alternative explanation that should also be investigated.","C":"The scenario is plausible via ACLs or disabled Block Public Access; it should not be dismissed without investigation."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-control-overview.html","tags":["s3","acl","bucket-policy","cross-account","security-audit"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-c5d6e7m9","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"S3 bucket policies, ACLs, and Object Lock","difficulty":"medium","question":"A legal department requires that specific S3 objects (contracts) cannot be deleted or modified for exactly 7 years, but the retention period can be extended by an authorized manager. Which Object Lock mode satisfies this?","options":{"A":"Compliance mode — cannot be shortened, but authorized IAM users with special permissions can extend the period","B":"Governance mode — allows users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission to modify retention; authorized managers can extend the period","C":"Legal Hold — prevents deletion until the hold is explicitly removed; no time-based retention needed","D":"Compliance mode — managers with the root account can extend the retention period"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Governance mode allows users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission to override, modify, or delete objects during the retention period. This makes it suitable when you need WORM protection for most users but want authorized managers to be able to extend retention periods or correct mistakes. Compliance mode does not allow any modifications, including by root.","distractors":{"A":"Compliance mode cannot be modified or shortened by any user, including root; only extending (never shortening) via specific API calls is allowed in Compliance mode, but even that requires careful understanding — Governance mode is the correct answer for manager-modifiable retention.","C":"Legal Hold has no time-based retention; it must be manually removed and does not enforce the '7 years exactly' business requirement described.","D":"Compliance mode does not allow the root account to extend retention in any standard way; Governance mode with s3:BypassGovernanceRetention is the appropriate choice for authorized exceptions."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-overview.html","tags":["s3","object-lock","governance-mode","compliance-mode","worm"],"verified":true},{"id":"02-d6e7f8n0","version":"2026-05","setId":"set-02-secure-architectures","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"S3 bucket policies, ACLs, and Object Lock","difficulty":"easy","question":"AWS recommends disabling S3 ACLs and using bucket policies instead. Which S3 setting enforces that all objects are owned by the bucket owner and ACLs are disabled?","options":{"A":"Bucket Policy Condition: StringEquals aws:PrincipalOrgID","B":"S3 Object Ownership setting: Bucket owner enforced","C":"S3 Block Public Access: BlockPublicAcls enabled","D":"S3 Versioning: Suspended"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The S3 Object Ownership 'Bucket owner enforced' setting disables ACLs entirely. All objects uploaded to the bucket are automatically owned by the bucket owner, regardless of the uploading account. Bucket policies become the sole access control mechanism. This is AWS's recommended modern approach for most S3 use cases.","distractors":{"A":"aws:PrincipalOrgID is a condition key in bucket policies to restrict access to organization members; it does not disable ACLs or enforce bucket owner ownership.","C":"BlockPublicAcls prevents public ACLs from being set but does not disable all ACLs; cross-account ACLs can still be set. 'Bucket owner enforced' is the complete solution.","D":"S3 Versioning controls object version history; it has no effect on ownership or ACLs."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/about-object-ownership.html","tags":["s3","object-ownership","acl","bucket-owner-enforced"],"verified":true}]`);export{e as default};